Abstract
 This project, undertaken during the Summer Internship Program 2026 at the Institute of Data Engineering, Analytics and Science (IDEAS) Foundation, explores practical applications of data analysis and unsupervised machine learning. The research is divided into two distinct components: an exploratory data analysis (EDA) of a coffee sales dataset and the implementation of clustering algorithms on the MNIST handwritten digits dataset. In the first phase, raw transactional data is ingested, cleaned, and structurally transformed to facilitate temporal analysis by converting date strings into structured datetime objects. Comprehensive descriptive statistics and data quality checks are performed to identify missing values, duplicate entries, and structural inconsistencies within the dataset. Subsequent data aggregation techniques are applied to uncover underlying business trends, evaluating metrics such as average yearly sales, peak revenue months, top-performing coffee variants, and sales distributions across different times of the day. To further demonstrate time-series manipulation capabilities, synthetic temporal data is generated and structured for potential predictive modeling. The second phase of the project shifts focus to computer vision and unsupervised learning using the standard high-dimensional MNIST dataset. A K-Means clustering algorithm is engineered to partition the unlabelled image features into ten distinct clusters, aiming to autonomously discover the natural groupings of the digits from 0 to 9. To rigorously evaluate the unsupervised model's performance against the ground truth labels, a label-mapping strategy is utilized to align arbitrary cluster assignments with their most frequent true digit classes. Finally, the clustering efficacy is quantified through the computation of a macro-averaged F1 score and a comprehensive confusion matrix, validating the algorithm's ability to discern complex image patterns without direct supervision. 

