3. Phase 1: Exploratory Data Analysis Pipeline
The following structured steps were executed to clean, pre-process, and analyze the transactional coffee dataset:
Data Ingestion & Inspection: The CSV file was loaded into a Pandas DataFrame. Initial quality checks confirmed the dataset contained 3,547 rows and 11 columns, with zero duplicate rows and zero missing values (nulls), indicating a highly structured raw dataset.
Descriptive Statistics: Basic statistical summaries (mean, standard deviation, minimum, maximum, and quartiles) were generated for all numerical features to understand the underlying distribution of sales and operational hours.
Pre-Processing and Type Conversion: The 'Date' feature, initially ingested as a string object, was programmatically converted to a standard datetime64[ns] object. This allowed for the extraction of specific temporal features: 'Month' and 'Year', which were appended as new columns.
Data Aggregation: The groupby() function was extensively utilized to extract business intelligence:
Temporal Trends: Calculated average revenue grouped by year and identified maximum revenue peaks grouped by month.
Product Performance: Summed total revenue across the 8 unique coffee types (e.g., Latte, Hot Chocolate, Americano).
Operational Trends: Calculated average sales segregated by the time of day (Morning, Afternoon, Night).
Synthetic Data Generation: To demonstrate time-series construction, an automated script utilizing pandas.date_range and numpy.random generated a synthetic dataset of 100 consecutive days mapped to randomized numerical values.
4. Phase 2: Machine Learning Model (K-Means Clustering)
The second phase focused on discovering inherent patterns within the MNIST handwritten digits dataset using unsupervised learning.
Model Selection: K-Means clustering was selected to group the pixel data autonomously. Because the objective was to test the algorithm's ability to organically discover the 10 distinct digit classes (0-9) without relying on labels, an unsupervised approach was required.
Data Splitting (Clarification): In traditional supervised machine learning, data is split into training and testing sets to prevent overfitting on known labels. However, because K-Means is an unsupervised algorithm operating here as an exploratory pattern-discovery tool, a traditional train/test split was not utilized. The model was fitted using the entirety of the feature matrix, X (1797 samples, 64 features).
Model Fitting: The K-Means algorithm was initialized with n_clusters=10 and random_state=42 to ensure reproducible results, and subsequently fitted to the feature data.
Validation and Evaluation: K-Means arbitrarily assigns numerical labels (0-9) to its clusters, which do not inherently align with the true digit labels. To validate the model:
Label Mapping: A script utilizing scipy.stats.mode was developed to map each arbitrary cluster label to the most frequent true ground-truth label (y) found within that cluster.
F1 Score: The macro-averaged F1 score was calculated (0.7895) to evaluate the harmonic mean of precision and recall evenly across all 10 classes.
Confusion Matrix: A $10 \times 10$ array was generated using sklearn.metrics.confusion_matrix and formatted into a DataFrame to visually map actual vs. predicted labels, highlighting exactly where the algorithm struggled to differentiate certain handwritten shapes.
